#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083324 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "179e0482-8d67-5e28-af2d-0d3aa7f0b770" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Given a Normal Distribution Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1103e6c4-8e5a-5d60-a8d6-1e582b31d670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357894} {:answer/id #uuid "4beccc4b-bd42-53f7-a6ef-d29833929fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358408} {:answer/id #uuid "58efc89e-0bb9-5250-bf2e-d9981f1e00d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358525} {:answer/id #uuid "ea0097db-0220-5610-aa9c-bf8496bfb7cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359833} {:answer/id #uuid "ee51d506-3a45-567d-86d2-6491d5d72676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58efc89e-0bb9-5250-bf2e-d9981f1e00d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358525} :answer-field/id #uuid "79790386-5ca3-5b7e-a326-b45ba6bd2fed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5563fda7-bd6a-55bd-869b-0a5d94077bb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-95024" :question/problem "The time that a particular laptop battery lasts is normally distributed with a mean of $32$ hours and a standard deviation of $4$ hours. What is the probability that a full charge lasts for $36$ hours or more? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the time the full charge lasts. Then we have $X∼N(32,{4}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X\\ge 36).$ Thus, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 36) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{36-32}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 1) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n1-Φ(1)=1-0.8413≈0.1587.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a full charge lasts $36$ hours or more is approximately $0.16.$"}]}
